## Claim
Text: "Wearing face masks will stop the spread of covid 19"
Claim author: Yinon Weiss
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/29/these-12-graphs-show-mask-mandates-do-nothing-to-stop-covid/

## Initial Q&A
### What was the scientific consensus as of October 29, 2020, regarding the effectiveness of face masks in preventing or reducing the spread of COVID-19?
Answer: As of the time of the claim (October 29, 2020), the PAHO/WHO guidelines—which were updated on June 19, 2020—indicated that face masks had been shown in scientific studies to be effective for protecting healthcare workers and reducing the risk of sick patients spreading the disease, while also noting that "wearing a face mask alone is not guaranteed to stop infections" and that "no preventive measure is 100% effective in preventing infection but practiced together they can reduce the chances of spreading infection." However, this source primarily reflects early-2020 guidance (February 28, 2020) and references subsequent June 2020 updates; it does not provide a comprehensive account of the scientific consensus specifically as of October 29, 2020.

Source URL: https://www.paho.org/en/news/28-2-2020-face-masks-during-outbreaks-who-when-where-and-how-use-them

### Did randomized controlled trials or large observational studies conducted before October 2020 find that face masks completely stop the transmission of COVID-19, or merely reduce it?
Answer: No. The systematic review (published April 2020, thus before October 2020) which examined 31 studies including 12 RCTs found that face masks only modestly or slightly reduce—not completely stop—transmission. Specifically, it found that "wearing a facemask may very slightly reduce the odds of developing ILI/respiratory symptoms, by around 6%" in community settings, and that "the odds of further household members becoming ill may be modestly reduced by around 19%" when both infected and uninfected household members wear masks. The authors explicitly concluded that "the evidence is not sufficiently strong to support widespread use of facemasks as a protective measure against COVID-19," indicating that masks were found to reduce but not completely stop transmission.

Source URL: https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1

### What was the official position of the World Health Organization (WHO) and the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) on face mask effectiveness against COVID-19 as of October 29, 2020?
Answer: As of the period leading up to October 29, 2020, the WHO (which updated its guidance on June 5, 2020) recommended that the general public wear clothmasks made from at least three layers of fabric "on public transport, in shops, or in other confined or crowded environments," and advised that people over 60 or with preexisting conditions wear medical masks in areas with community transmission, though WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus cautioned that "Masks alone will not protect you from Covid-19." The U.S. CDC, which had updated its guidance on April 3, 2020, recommended "wearing cloth face coverings in public settings where other social distancing measures are difficult to maintain" but had not made masks a requirement.

Source URL: https://www.vox.com/2020/6/6/21282108/masks-for-covid-19-world-health-organization-guidelines-cloth-n95

### Does the Federalist article by Yinon Weiss dated October 29, 2020, titled "These 12 Graphs Show Mask Mandates Do Nothing To Stop COVID," argue that mask mandates do not stop COVID-19 spread, or that masks themselves do not stop COVID-19 spread?
Answer: Based on the article's title, "These 12 Graphs Show Mask Mandates Do Nothing To Stop COVID," the Federalist article by Yinon Weiss argues that **mask mandates** do not stop COVID-19 spread, not that masks themselves do not stop the spread. However, the search result from AARP does not contain specific content from the Federalist article itself, so this assessment is based solely on the title provided in the Claim record rather than direct evidence from the article's text.

Source URL: https://www.aarp.org/health/healthy-living/info-2020/states-mask-mandates-coronavirus.html

### How does the effectiveness of face masks against COVID-19 vary by mask type (N95, surgical, cloth) and by setting (indoor, outdoor, healthcare, community)?
Answer: Based on the systematic review, mask effectiveness varies substantially by type and setting. **By mask type**, N95 respirators provide the highest protection (>95% filtration efficiency for small aerosols 0.08–0.22 µm), followed by surgical masks (~55% for small particles, 94% in some studies), with cloth masks being the least effective (2–38% for small particles, though multi-layered cotton combinations with silk/chiffon/flannel can reach >90% for larger particles). **By setting**, N95 and surgical masks are recommended for healthcare workers in hospital settings, where cloth masks are explicitly *not* recommended due to inadequate protection; in community settings, cloth masks offer minimal protection during casual contacts but moderate protection in household contacts when both infected and exposed individuals wear them, and may serve as a complementary measure in crowded indoor or outdoor public spaces where physical distancing is difficult—though they should always be combined with hand hygiene, social distancing, and respiratory etiquette.

Source URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7497125/

